WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.740
Nicole: CPD refers to the process
of developing, maintaining and also

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.090
documenting your skills, knowledge
and experience within your profession

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:10.589
and throughout your career.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.230
Abraham: CPD can be a structured
process to help ensure that standards

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.129
are kept high and consistent.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.740
It allows for greater commitment
and engagement and the sharing

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.940
of best practice and support
across an organisation.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.560
Nicole: CPD may be conducted internally
or by external specialist companies

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.430
who can deliver specific training.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.900
Abraham: In cyber security, CPD
is very important as the threat

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:37.690
landscape is constantly changing.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.120
Many professionals find it hard
to stay on top of their roles,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.580
whilst keeping up to date with new
techniques and malware being developed.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:49.920
Nicole: This is why employers should
always set time aside for regular

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.229
CPD training even for their top
professionals and not just rely on

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.360
employees to keep their knowledge
up to date, on their own time.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.250
Abraham: Cyber security teams in
turn can also deliver CPD training

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.059
to all other staff to help keep
an organisation safe and secure.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.300
Nicole: As well as regular structured CPD
training, cyber security professionals

00:01:11.359 --> 00:01:14.640
should look to other ways of keeping up
to date with their industry knowledge.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.890
There is no one way of doing
this and there are many sources

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.720
that can offer information both
technical and news oriented.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.950
Abraham: Technology websites and
magazines may cover aspects of

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.060
security on a regular basis, so it
is worthwhile for those involved in

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.980
cyber security to know where to look
for the most up to date information.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:41.119
Sites such as NCSC, Wired, Computer
Weekly The Hacker News, etc.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.249
It’s worth checking out their websites!

00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:48.220
Nicole: There are also many companies
that sell security software to home and

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:53.149
business users who also regularly update
their websites with emerging security

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.179
threats and how to overcome them.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.750
Abraham: But remember their
main aim is to sell software!

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:02.929
Nicole: Many security professionals
have their own blogs or websites,

00:02:03.349 --> 00:02:05.349
but you should be careful
that they are legitimate.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:10.139
Abraham: As a cyber security professional,
you will probably get to interact with

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.960
others in your field meeting them at
training sessions, conferences or online.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.680
Building up a network is time
consuming but a great way to share

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.420
information as well as helping
tomove forward your career.

00:02:23.039 --> 00:02:25.380
You never know who is
looking for new employees!

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:29.150
Nicole: Social media can sometimes
be helpful if you know where to

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.829
find specific forums for cyber
security news and information but

00:02:33.929 --> 00:02:35.520
should not be always relied on!

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.950
Abraham: Educating yourself on the dark
web can also give up to date information

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.330
on what malware is available and what
organisations have recently been hacked.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.609
Nicole: And finally, there are the
good old face to face as well as

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.110
online cyber security events such as
conferences, lectures, discussions,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:57.530
workshops, networking events,
educational courses and boot camps.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.980
Abraham: These may cost to attend but
employers will often have a budget to

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:04.710
cover these especially when used for CPD.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:11.170
Nicole: Cyber security and the threat
landscape are constantly changing and CPD

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:15.229
is an important way to keep up to date
with all the changes and keep pace with

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.469
new emerging threats and new technologies.

00:03:18.359 --> 00:03:22.079
Abraham: Cyber security teams who fail
to keep up to date or fall behind with

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.520
their CPD, leave themselves open to
coming a victim of the latest attack

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.650
and leave their organisation vulnerable.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:33.379
Nicole: On a personal level CPD can help
you grow and develop your professional

00:03:33.389 --> 00:03:37.549
career, but it can also help those
you work for, to benefit from the

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.690
information and new skills you gain.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.140
Abraham: Further learning can
significantly improve your knowledge

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.720
and skills and can often help to
meet regulatory requirements for

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.890
your organisation, but don’t just
think of CPD as something you have

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.210
to do, embrace the opportunity.

00:03:53.529 --> 00:03:57.710
Nicole: Also remember to let your employer
know how further training for you, can

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:01.829
help everyone, as you can disseminate
that information and help to keep

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.140
the whole organisation cyber secure.

